<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · river</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ticino River</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ticino">Ticino</a> is a river that rises in the Swiss <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alps">Alps</a>, flows through the canton of Ticino and Lake Maggiore, and then runs south through <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lombardy">Lombardy</a> to join the Po just below Pavia. Its lower course formed a frontier between Lombardy and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/piedmont">Piedmont</a>, and in 218 BCE <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hannibal">Hannibal</a> defeated the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a> in a cavalry battle near its banks.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ticino-river.jpg" alt="Ticino River" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Ruaro Matteo · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Twilight_of_the_old_ways.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ticino">Ticino</a> is the most important perennial left-bank tributary of the Po, about 248 km long, and it gave its name to the Swiss canton through which its upper part flows. It is one of the four major rivers that rise in the Gotthard region, with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhone">Rhône</a>, Reuss and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhine">Rhine</a>. It begins in the Val Bedretto, on the border between the cantons of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/valais">Valais</a> and Ticino just below the Nufenen Pass, where glaciers and many torrents feed it. Its name may have meant the runner, from a Proto-Indo-European root meaning to run or flow.</p><p>From Val Bedretto it flows north-east to Airolo, where the southern portals of the Gotthard Railway Tunnel and Gotthard Road Tunnel lie, then south-east through the Valle Leventina, with two narrow gorges. At Biasca it receives the Brenno, and near Bellinzona the Moesa. It crosses the flat Piano di Magadino, built from its own sediments over ten thousand years, and enters Lake Maggiore at 193 metres, the lowest point of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/switzerland">Switzerland</a>. It leaves the lake at Sesto Calende, passes about 20 km east of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/milan">Milan</a> and joins the Po a few kilometres below Pavia.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The Gallic leader <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bellovesus">Bellovesus</a>, known only from tradition, was said to have defeated the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/etruscans">Etruscans</a> by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ticino">Ticino</a> in about 600 BCE. The river gave its name to the Battle of Ticinus, fought in November 218 BCE between the Carthaginian forces of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hannibal">Hannibal</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a> under Publius Cornelius Scipio; it was the first battle of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-punic-war">Second Punic War</a>.</p><p>In the Middle Ages Pavia, first capital of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-the-lombards">Kingdom of the Lombards</a> and then of the kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>, was a hub of communications and trade between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/venice">Venice</a> and the Po valley thanks to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ticino">Ticino</a>. Its covered bridge, the ponte Coperto, was until the 19th century the only brick bridge across the river between Lake Maggiore and the Po.</p><p>The Visconti built a wooden bridge at Vigevano in the early 14th century, which the Pavia fleet burned in 1315 and again in 1356, after Luchino Visconti had rebuilt it; it was never rebuilt. For much of the later <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">Middle Ages</a> and the early modern period the river lay in the Duchy of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/milan">Milan</a>, but the upper part as far as Bellinzona fell to the Old Swiss Confederacy in 1500, and as far as the shores of Lake Maggiore in 1513, as a result of the Swiss campaigns in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italian-wars">Italian Wars</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>The stretch between Lake Maggiore and the confluence with the Po is the Parco naturale lombardo della Valle del <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ticino">Ticino</a>, a nature reserve included by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco">UNESCO</a> in its World Network of Biosphere Reserves. Towns on the river or its lake include Bellinzona and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/locarno">Locarno</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/switzerland">Switzerland</a> and Stresa, Vigevano and Pavia in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Ticino (river)” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Pavia controlled the confluence of the <b>Ticino</b> and the Po and much of the Lomellina.</blockquote><a href="/state/commune-of-pavia">Commune of Pavia</a></li></ul></section>
